cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this teaching material, or the models in it, please cite it as below."
title: "Teaching notebooks for IE 3315, IE 5301 and REE 4301"
abstract: >-
  Seventeen executed teaching notebooks and an accompanying Python package,
  organised by subject across three courses in industrial engineering and energy
  systems modelling. Each notebook builds its model by hand and then asserts
  agreement with the packaged implementation, so the two cannot drift apart
  silently.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Jones
    given-names: Erick C.
    name-suffix: Jr.
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0559-4699"
    affiliation: "University of Texas at Arlington"
repository-code: "https://github.com/sear-labs/teaching-code"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - teaching material
  - operations research
  - optimization
  - energy systems
  - industrial engineering
  - jupyter notebooks
# No doi: yet, and that is deliberate rather than an oversight -- a DOI cannot
# exist before the first release. The order is forced:
#   1. this file, with no doi                         <- you are here
#   2. connect the repo in Zenodo, tag a release
#   3. Zenodo mints a VERSION doi and a CONCEPT doi
#   4. add the CONCEPT doi here and commit
# The concept DOI always resolves to the latest version and never changes, so
# step 4 is a one-time edit and not a per-release chore. See the Code Standard,
# Part 5, "Every published repo carries a licence AND a citation".
